# Trusted device estate resilience

`DOCTOR-ACCESS-TRUSTED-DEVICE-ESTATE-RESILIENCE-1`. Read with rules 151–157,
which this extends rather than replaces. Rule 152 owns the trusted PATH, rule 156
owns whether the path has been WALKED. This one owns whether the HARDWARE
survives losing a tablet.

## The defect this sprint found

`spare_device_available_per_branch` was a declared prerequisite of global doctor
enforcement from Phase 3.5 and **had no implementation for its entire life** — a
string in `android_release.enforcement.global_prerequisites` and a hand-signed
boolean beside it. `Phase4aPilotPreparationScanner::globalPrerequisiteCheck()`
asserts that somebody signed; it says so in its own docblock and it is right to.
The consequence was that nothing in the system could tell the signer whether what
they were signing was true.

`doctor:estate-resilience` now measures the measurable half. It does not sign.

---

## ER-R1 — A device's branch is not an authorization authority

`mst_doctor_devices.branch_id` says where the hardware physically lives. It has
never decided which doctors may use it, and no code may start making it decide.
Cross-branch trusted-device use is valid and PROVEN: every ceremony in the fleet
readiness campaign ran on one tablet at SPN4, including for doctors homed
elsewhere.

Per-branch device counts are **operational resilience** — can this room keep
working when its tablet breaks — and never an access boundary. Any report that
prints them must say so on the same screen.

## ER-R2 — Local device coverage is resilience, not authorization

A branch that homes doctors and holds no eligible tablet has a **physical
availability** gap. Its doctors are not locked out; they can authenticate on a
tablet elsewhere. Never describe or fix this as an authorization problem, and
never widen an authorization to close it.

## ER-R3 — Credential scope is derived from source, never assumed

`trx_doctor_device_webauthn_credentials` has `doctor_device_id` and **no doctor
or user column**, and deliberately never will. The credential belongs to the
TABLET; the clinician is identified by the password step.

Therefore **one credential enrolment per tablet covers every doctor authorized on
it.** Do not schedule one ceremony per doctor per tablet. Before relying on this
in a future sprint, re-derive it from the login path rather than from this rule.

## ER-R4 — A revoked device is never capacity

Not eligible, not a spare, not credential coverage, and never able to close an
authorization gap — however recently it worked and however many historical
authorizations name it. A repaired unit **enrols as a new device**; the old row
stays revoked forever. Eligibility is asked of the MODEL (`isActive()` and
`isCryptographicallyVerified()`), never restated as a status string, so the three
engines that ask it cannot drift into disagreeing about which hardware counts.

## ER-R5 — Changing the eligible estate obliges an authorization reconciliation

The authorization target is `eligible doctors x eligible trusted devices` and is
recomputed from the CURRENT estate. Adding a tablet therefore **opens** a gap
rather than closing one, and every doctor must be authorized on it before
`authorization_coverage` returns to PASS. Never carry a previous matrix figure
forward — a stale `45/45` is how a new tablet becomes invisible.

## ER-R6 — An input the system does not hold produces UNVERIFIED, never PASS

The only definition of *spare* the codebase owns is
`devices per branch = concurrent Doctor stations + 1 spare`, and the station
count is a fact about a room. `UNVERIFIED` is a first-class verdict beside
PASS/FAIL: it never contributes to a passing tally and always names the missing
input.

What IS decidable without the count is the lower bound — any station count ≥ 1
needs at least two eligible devices — so 0 or 1 eligible devices FAILS outright.
Undecidability begins at two.

**Treatment rooms are not the station count.** `mst_clinic_rooms` with
`type = 'treatment_room'` is branch-scoped and populated, and it is the obvious
candidate — an adversarial review found this sprint had wrongly claimed no
candidate existed. It is read and REPORTED as an advisory sizing reference, and
it decides nothing: a room inventory is not peak concurrent staffed stations
(three rooms with one doctor on shift is one station; one room with two chairs is
two). Do not promote it to the input without measuring the difference.

The count has a home: `android_release.enforcement.concurrent_doctor_stations_per_branch`,
keyed by branch code, **shipping empty**. A declared value below one is treated
as undeclared rather than honoured, so a typo cannot manufacture headroom. An
empty estate FAILS rather than passing.

## ER-R7 — An attestation is a signature, not a measurement, and this engine does not sign

`doctor:estate-resilience` reports MEASURED beside ATTESTED and raises a finding
when they contradict. It does not gate on the attestation, does not write it, and
cannot be satisfied by it. Equally, a signature cannot rescue a measured FAIL.

**Never attest a prerequisite because a sprint intends to satisfy it.** Attest
only after the measured condition is true, and cite the evidence.

## ER-R8 — Estate-resilience GO does not authorize global activation

A PASS means the hardware survives one loss. It does not arm single-session, does
not make branch lock effective, does not widen the pilot cohort, and does not
permit fleet-wide enforcement. `authorizes_activation` is a literal `false` in the
report and must stay one.

Single-session must remain OFF and global enforcement OFF throughout any estate
provisioning work.

## ER-R9 — The report is read-only and stays that way

No write method may be added to this engine or its reads. A resilience engine
that can write is one that can manufacture its own capacity — the same reason
rule 156 forbids it of the readiness engine.

## ER-R10 — Compose the sibling engine, and name the agreement gate for exactly what it compares

This engine composes the fleet engine rather than re-deriving the authorization
matrix, because a second calculator is a second thing to keep in step. The price
is two estate reads, so the report carries an `eligible_device_set_agreement`
gate that compares the eligible device id lists and degrades to UNVERIFIED when
they differ. The sibling engine has already shipped one mixed-snapshot bug; do
not remove this gate to save a query.

**And name it for exactly what it compares.** It was first called
`estate_snapshot_agreement`, and its PASS text said the two engines had read the
same hardware. They have not been shown to: each issues its own lock read and
its own credential read, so a lock moved or a credential revoked between them
produces a two-moment report with an identical device id list and a green gate.
Narrowing a verdict obliges narrowing its MESSAGE too, or the old claim survives
where operators actually read it.

## ER-R11 — The branch universe is a union, and naming a branch code in a gate is forbidden

Scope is every branch that **either** holds a registered Doctor device **or**
homes a doctor. Today those two halves disagree — one branch holds a tablet and
homes nobody, another homes two doctors and holds nothing — and either half alone
would hide a real branch.

Gates are generalised, never written against a branch code. A gate naming `TLK1`
stops applying the day a branch is renamed, and this estate has already renamed a
branch code once.

## ER-R13 — Every aggregate verdict fails closed, and every population is named

`worst()` returned PASS by fallthrough for any string outside its vocabulary —
`'BANANA'`, `'pass'`, `null` — so a typo or a future fourth status would have
read as satisfied. It is now an allow-list: anything unrecognised is FAIL.

Name the POPULATION a gate measures, and make an empty one FAIL. Taking the
worst over every branch let an estate of only unstaffed branches report
`spare_device_available_per_branch` as PASS while holding no usable tablet, and
the attestation cross-check reads its verdict from that gate — so a signed
`true` came back as "no contradiction". The spare requirement's population is
branches that home doctors.

## ER-R14 — Count live doctors, never lock rows

`Doctor` is soft-deleted and `mst_doctor_branch_locks` has a `restrictOnDelete`
FK, so a retired doctor keeps their home lock forever. Counting rows sizes a
branch for somebody who no longer practises and disagrees with every engine that
resolves doctors through a soft-delete-scoped query — silently, because the
device-set agreement gate does not compare doctors. Exclude them, and REPORT the
orphan rows rather than swallowing them.

## ER-R12 — Do not duplicate a security decision to measure it

Credential **admissibility** (user-verified, backup-eligible, device-bound) is
the login gate's decision and the provisioning engine's report. This engine
answers the narrower question it can answer honestly — does the tablet carry an
UNREVOKED credential — using the model's own predicates, and says which question
it answered. Re-implementing the admissibility policy here would create a second
copy that eventually disagrees with the gate it describes.
